use std
into lodgen.amni

Imports: math, gfx.gltf and gfx.linalg. Namespace lodgen. Builds cheaper level-of-detail copies of loaded glTF assets by vertex clustering: the mesh's bounding box is cut into a grid of equal cubes, every vertex in a cube snaps to one existing vertex of that cube, and triangles that collapse are dropped. No vertices are made, so a reduced primitive keeps its vertex range and only gets a new index list. Vertex positions come as `pos`, an f32 slice, with `stride` floats per vertex and the x, y, z of vertex v at `pos[v * stride + at]`, `+ 1`, `+ 2`. Triangles are u32 vertex ids, three per triangle, all below nv. Geometry math is done in `real`.

`mix(k int) int` scrambles an int key for hashing: wrapping multiply by 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15, then xor with itself shifted right (logical) by 29, and that result made non-negative by and-ing with the largest positive int.

`grid_pass(tris, pos, stride int, at int, nv int, grid int, out) int` (tris: read-only u32 slice; pos: read-only f32 slice; out: growable u32 array by reference) clusters with `grid` cubes along the longest side of the box and writes the surviving triangles to `out` (cleared first), returning how many there are.
- Return 0 when nv or the triangle count is 0. Mark which vertices some triangle uses; the box lo and hi cover only used vertices.
- The cube size is the box's largest side (at least 1e-12) divided by grid. Each axis has cells equal to the floor of its side over the size, plus one, capped at grid and at least 1. A used vertex's cell on an axis is the floor of its offset from lo over the size, clamped into that axis's cells; its cell id is (ix times the y cells plus iy) times the z cells plus iz.
- Give every distinct cell id of the used vertices a dense slot number in first-seen order, using an open-addressing hash table of cell ids (two int arrays for keys and slots, size the smallest power of two at least 2 * nv + 16, empty keys -1, linear probing from `mix(key)` and-ed with size minus one). Keep the slot of each used vertex.
- Each slot has a quadric of ten reals, all zero to start (aa, ab, ac, ad, bb, bc, bd, cc, cd, dd of a plane a, b, c, d). For each triangle with corners p0, p1, p2: n2 is the cross product of p1 minus p0 and p2 minus p0; when its length w is above 1e-30, the plane is the unit normal n2 over w with d the negated dot of that normal with p0, and w times that plane's ten products is added to the quadric of each distinct slot among its three corners.
- A used vertex's error is its slot's quadric evaluated at its position (x, y, z, 1): aa x x + 2 ab x y + 2 ac x z + 2 ad x + bb y y + 2 bc y z + 2 bd y + cc z z + 2 cd z + dd. Each slot's chosen vertex is its used vertex with the smallest error, the lowest id on ties.
- For each triangle in order, its new corners are the chosen vertices of its corners' slots. Skip it when two new corners are the same. Rotate the three, keeping their order around, so the smallest id comes first; skip it when that same rotated triple was already written (a second open-addressing hash set over the int key (first times nv plus second) times nv plus third, sized the smallest power of two at least 2 * triangle count + 16, same probing). Otherwise push the three ids to out.

`reduce(tris, pos, stride int, at int, nv int, target int, out) int` (same argument kinds as `grid_pass`) writes at most about `target` triangles to `out` (cleared first) and returns their count. When the triangle count is at most target, or target is below 1, copy tris to out unchanged. Otherwise binary search the grid between 1 and 1024, at most 11 rounds: try the middle (rounded up), keep it as the new low when `grid_pass` gives at most target triangles, else the new high is the middle minus one. Finish with `grid_pass` at the low grid; when that gives no triangles, copy tris unchanged instead.

`tris(asset int) int` is the asset's triangle count: the sum over every primitive of every mesh in the asset's mesh span of its index count over three.

`make(asset int, keep f32) int` adds a reduced copy of a loaded asset to gltf's arrays and returns its asset id; with keep at 1 or above it returns asset unchanged. The copy shares the original's vertices, materials, textures, images, skins and animations spans.
- For each mesh of the asset's mesh span in order, add a new mesh whose primitives are new primitives appended in the same order, keeping the mesh's weights and name. A primitive copy keeps every field. Its triangle target is the larger of 12 and its triangle count times keep, rounded. When it is skinned, has morph targets or its count is at most the target, it keeps the original index range. Otherwise `reduce` its indices (gltf's indices from its first index, its index count of them) over its vertices (gltf's verts from its first vertex times `gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS` for its vertex count of vertices, stride `gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`, at `gltf::AT_POS`, nv its vertex count), append the result to gltf's indices, and point the copy's first index and index count at it.
- Then copy every node of the asset's node span in order, with its mesh moved to the matching new mesh (by its offset in the asset's mesh span; -1 stays -1), its parent moved by the same offset as the nodes (-1 stays -1), and its children a new span of gltf's ints holding each child moved the same way.
- The new asset's roots are a new span of gltf's ints holding each root moved the same way. Its mesh and node spans cover the new meshes and nodes, and ok stays true.
